  "hmrc:Contents!A3": {
   "source_id": "hmrc",
   "sheet": "Contents",
   "cell": "A3",
   "value": "Table 2.4 shows how the distributions of income and Income Tax liabilities have changed between 1999 to 2000 to the current tax year. Income distribution provides one measure of income inequality, while the shares of total Income Tax liabilities reflect the progressivity of the Income Tax system. The table also shows the income breakpoints for each percentile group, meaning the point at which the next percentile group begins. Table 2.4 does not provide a complete picture of individual income inequality in the UK due to the exclusion of those who don’t pay Income Tax, and because the SPI records only include incomes that are assessable for tax (for example, a range of non-taxable social security benefits, some tax credits and non-taxable savings from ISAs are not included).  \n\nIncome Tax payers are ranked based on total income assessable for tax (earnings, savings and dividend income) before any deductions and tax allowances (such as pension contributions), and then divided into percentile groups ranged on income. For projection estimates for tax years 2023 and 2024 onwards each income component is grown at the same rate for all individuals in each projection year except for pay, where differential earnings growth across the pay distribution is consistent with past trends including the impact of forestalling from previous policy changes (for more details see the Supporting Documentation).\n  \nPrior to 2016 to 2017, total income includes the amount of dividends plus dividend tax credit (one ninth of the dividend), the grossed dividend, and Income Tax is charged on the grossed dividend. The tax due can be satisfied (in part) by the notional tax credit (10% of the grossed dividend).  The table reflects the grossed dividend in total income and shows the income tax liability before the tax credit is offset. From 2016 to 2017 the dividend tax credit was abolished and effective dividend tax rates were increased by 7.5% and a £5,000 Dividend Allowance was introduced. This affects the measure of total income and leads to a discontinuity in the basis on which tax liabilities are presented between the years up to and including 2015 to 2016 and 2016 to 2017. As a result the share of incomes and tax liabilities are not directly comparable. Data shown refers to taxpayers only. Figures for the 2008 to 2009 tax year are not currently available. ",
   "text": "Table 2.4 shows how the distributions of income and Income Tax liabilities have changed between 1999 to 2000 to the current tax year. Income distribution provides one measure of income inequality, while the shares of total Income Tax liabilities reflect the progressivity of the Income Tax system. The table also shows the income breakpoints for each percentile group, meaning the point at which the next percentile group begins. Table 2.4 does not provide a complete picture of individual income inequality in the UK due to the exclusion of those who don’t pay Income Tax, and because the SPI records only include incomes that are assessable for tax (for example, a range of non-taxable social security benefits, some tax credits and non-taxable savings from ISAs are not included).  \n\nIncome Tax payers are ranked based on total income assessable for tax (earnings, savings and dividend income) before any deductions and tax allowances (such as pension contributions), and then divided into percentile groups ranged on income. For projection estimates for tax years 2023 and 2024 onwards each income component is grown at the same rate for all individuals in each projection year except for pay, where differential earnings growth across the pay distribution is consistent with past trends including the impact of forestalling from previous policy changes (for more details see the Supporting Documentation).\n  \nPrior to 2016 to 2017, total income includes the amount of dividends plus dividend tax credit (one ninth of the dividend), the grossed dividend, and Income Tax is charged on the grossed dividend. The tax due can be satisfied (in part) by the notional tax credit (10% of the grossed dividend).  The table reflects the grossed dividend in total income and shows the income tax liability before the tax credit is offset. From 2016 to 2017 the dividend tax credit was abolished and effective dividend tax rates were increased by 7.5% and a £5,000 Dividend Allowance was introduced. This affects the measure of total income and leads to a discontinuity in the basis on which tax liabilities are presented between the years up to and including 2015 to 2016 and 2016 to 2017. As a result the share of incomes and tax liabilities are not directly comparable. Data shown refers to taxpayers only. Figures for the 2008 to 2009 tax year are not currently available. ",
   "formula": null
  },
